{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_glossary_glossary", "label": "Kosmos Glossary", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_nebula", "label": "Nebula (the microkernel)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Threads, address spaces, IPC, capabilities and nothing else. What kernel/, arch/ and hal/ build into. It does not know what a file, a pixel or a network is."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_kosmos", "label": "Kosmos (the operating system)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Nebula plus the servers, the namespace, the desktop and the applications. What somebody uses. The two names are not interchangeable."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_server", "label": "Server - a process that owns something", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The definition is about ownership rather than code. It was handed a capability nobody else holds and rents the thing out through a message protocol. Mechanically serve(endpoint, state, handlers); what makes it worth the boundary is that only the disk server can corrupt the disk, because nothing else can name the disk."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_kit", "label": "Kit - C that runs inside your own process", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "use(\"/kits/compress\") hands back a table of C functions compiled into your address space. No process, no message, no ownership: calling it is a function call. Today /kits/compress, /kits/pdf, /kits/gl and /kits/console."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_kit_vs_server", "label": "A kit is code you run; a server is someone you ask", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The distinction that does the most work in the whole vocabulary. Inflate is a kit because it computes; the disk is a server because it owns."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_console_kit", "label": "/kits/console - the one protocol with two implementations", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A terminal window mounts itself as its child's console, so an application answers the same ABI the server does. The kit is where that wire layout is compiled once rather than living as a format string in the namespace and a second copy inside the terminal."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_library", "label": "Library - the same position, in Lua", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "use(\"/lib/ui.lua\") loads Lua source into the caller's own environment. ui, panel, pdf, kfs."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_program", "label": "Program - console-based", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "It prints, it reads lines, it lives in /bin and you type its name at the prompt: ls, cat, htop, stress."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_app", "label": "App - graphical", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "It opens a window, is driven with the pointer and appears in the Deskbar. It says so with -- kosmos: application in its opening comment, which is how the program store knows to list it."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_tool", "label": "Tool - host-side, never runs on Kosmos", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "tools/ holds the test runners, the benchmark harness, kfs.lua writing a disk image from the Mac, bdf2c.py converting a font. They are the workshop, not the machine."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_driver", "label": "Driver", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The code that touches hardware, in hal/. One per board, behind a common interface."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_language_split", "label": "Which language, and why", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "C for anything that touches hardware, defines the isolation boundary, or sits where a pause would be felt. Lua for anything whose bugs can only kill their own process. The servers row used to read 'servers on the frame or packet path' - a per-server judgement that failed, because the audio server sits on the period path and nobody recognised it as a third case. A rule that needs you to spot a third case will miss the fourth, so the layer decides now."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_jitter_argument", "label": "The argument for C is jitter rather than speed", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Structure-shaped Lua costs about 2%; a loop over bytes costs 30%; the PDF scanner was 110x. But what decides a server is gc_pause_max, about 1.25 ms arriving when the collector decides, against a 16 ms frame. No amount of optimising the Lua removes that pause."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_shape_of_the_bug", "label": "What argues for Lua now is the shape of the bug", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A Lua module cannot have a buffer overflow. Isolation is identical either way, since both are EL0 processes behind an address space, but one failure is a stack trace and the other is an evening."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_diskfs_exception", "label": "diskfs is the exception, and not for language reasons", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "kfs.lua runs on the host too, which is what tests the journal without booting a machine."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_microkernel", "label": "Microkernel", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The kernel does the minimum - threads, memory, IPC - and everything else runs in userland processes. The opposite is monolithic."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_ipc", "label": "IPC", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_synchronous_ipc", "label": "Synchronous IPC / rendezvous", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The sender blocks until the receiver is ready, with no buffering in the kernel. Simpler and faster than asynchronous, at the cost of code being harder to write - and coroutines solve that."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_capability", "label": "Capability", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A permission that is also the only way to name something. If you were not handed the capability, you cannot even name the resource."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_endpoint", "label": "Endpoint", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_address_space", "label": "Address space", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_context_switch", "label": "Context switch", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Written in assembly, and getting it wrong corrupts things that only show up five functions later."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_smp", "label": "SMP", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_ipi", "label": "IPI - inter-processor interrupt", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Needed for cross-core IPC and for TLB shootdown."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_namespace", "label": "Namespace", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The map of paths to resources a process sees. In Plan 9 and in Kosmos it is per-process, not global. What is not mounted does not exist."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_9p", "label": "9P", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Plan 9's protocol: every resource, local or remote, is spoken to the same way. The Kosmos protocol is 9P with typed records instead of byte streams."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_mount", "label": "Mount", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_exception_levels", "label": "EL0 / EL1 / EL2 / EL3", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Which level you are handed depends on the machine and its options, so read CurrentEL rather than assuming."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_mmu", "label": "MMU", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_long_descriptor_page_tables", "label": "Long-descriptor page tables", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "ARMv8's format: four levels, 64-bit entries. ARMv6 (Pi 1) uses short-descriptor, two levels and 32-bit entries - a different format entirely."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_tlb", "label": "TLB", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_tlb_shootdown", "label": "TLB shootdown", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "On AArch64 the broadcast is done in hardware (TLBI with an IS suffix), which saves considerable work compared to x86."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_memory_barrier", "label": "Memory barrier", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "dsb, dmb, isb. Necessary because AArch64 has a weak memory model."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_weak_memory_model", "label": "Weak memory model", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "x86 has TSO, which is far more conservative. Code that works on x86 can fail on ARM without barriers, once every thousand boots."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_mmio", "label": "MMIO", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_esr_elr_far", "label": "ESR / ELR / FAR", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The three together turn a hang into a useful message: what kind of exception, which instruction, and what address."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_gic", "label": "GIC", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "GICv2 and GICv3 are quite different from each other; the Pi 5 and QEMU virt use v3."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_tpidr_el1", "label": "TPIDR_EL1", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A register reserved by convention to point at the per-CPU struct. The glossary claims using it from day one makes SMP cheap later; smp.md records that it has never been written."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_coroutine", "label": "Coroutine", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Concurrency without parallelism and without locks. It is what makes it possible to write sequential code over synchronous IPC."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_lua_state", "label": "lua_State", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "One per process in Kosmos."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_gc_modes", "label": "Incremental / generational GC", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Incremental takes steps bounded in work, not in time, so it can blow the frame budget. Generational usually does better with many short-lived tables."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_longjmp", "label": "longjmp", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Lua uses it for error handling, and it is the reason Rust does not fit well as the kernel language here: destructors never run."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_freestanding", "label": "Freestanding", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_app_server", "label": "app_server (BeOS)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The BeOS process handling windows, decoration and compositing. Kosmos has its equivalent."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_bmessage", "label": "BLooper / BHandler / BMessage", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A BLooper was a thread with a message queue; a BMessage a typed dictionary. In Kosmos they are coroutines and Lua tables."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_bview", "label": "BView", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A rectangle that knows how to draw itself and receive events, with coordinates relative to its parent. The Kosmos equivalent is the UI kit's view."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_follow_modes", "label": "Follow modes", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Each view declares which edges of the parent it stays attached to. Fifty lines, against the thousands of a constraint solver."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_live_query", "label": "Live query", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Registering a predicate over attributes and receiving a message when the result set changes. No polling. It is the BeOS feature nobody replicated."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_replicant", "label": "Replicant", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A view dragged from one app into another that keeps working. In BeOS a binary add-on; in Kosmos Lua source plus state plus declared capabilities."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_attributes", "label": "Attributes", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Typed metadata alongside a file, indexed by the filesystem. What makes live queries possible."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_framebuffer", "label": "Framebuffer", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_backbuffer", "label": "Backbuffer", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The framebuffer is usually uncached device memory, where writing is 10-50x slower than to normal RAM. Hence drawing into cached RAM and following with a single blit."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_blit", "label": "Blit", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_pitch", "label": "Pitch / stride", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Almost never width * 4, because the firmware aligns it."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_damage_tracking", "label": "Damage tracking", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_compositing", "label": "Compositing", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_fixed_point_16_16", "label": "16.16 fixed point", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Doom used it, and in Kosmos it is necessary until lazy FP save exists."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_surface", "label": "Surface", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A handle to pixel memory shared between an app and the app server. The justified exception to the drawing-command model. Used by Paint, the 3D demo and Doom."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_drawing_commands", "label": "Drawing commands (model B)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "An app sends tables describing what to draw instead of writing pixels. The Kosmos default model."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_hot_reload_level_1", "label": "Hot reload level 1 - removed", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Removed September 2026 when the last Lua server became C. There is no dynamic linking, so nothing that runs here can have its code replaced. The honest word is removed rather than outranked."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_hot_reload_level_2", "label": "Hot reload level 2 - supervised restart", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A server dies and a supervisor relaunches it; clients reconnect through the namespace. It never depended on what language a server was written in, which is why it survived."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_doomgeneric", "label": "doomgeneric", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The Doom port that separated the engine from the platform. Five functions to implement."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_rp1", "label": "RP1 - the Pi 5 southbridge", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "USB, Ethernet and the header GPIO hang off it over PCIe. It is what makes the Pi 5 hostile to bare metal."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_mailbox", "label": "Mailbox (Pi firmware)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_glossary_endianness", "label": "Big-endian / little-endian", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "PowerPC is big-endian; ARM and x86 are little-endian. Mixing them forces you to define an explicit wire format."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_open_firmware", "label": "Open Firmware", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The firmware on PowerPC Macs: a Forth environment that stays alive after loading your kernel and exposes a console and a device tree."}, {"id": "docs_glossary_repl", "label": "REPL", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/glossary.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "In Kosmos it is the shell and the way the system is modified while running."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_glossary_glossary", "target": "docs_glossary_kit_vs_server", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence